SPL -- the print spooler's queue directory==========================================splman (the manager), splprt (the printer process) and splstat (the statusreport) all open /DD/SPL/splq.  Without the directory and the file they eachstopped at error 216, path-not-found, and said nothing.splq is here and EMPTY: an empty queue is the correct state for a spoolerwith nothing to print.THE CLIENTS WANT A MODULE, NOT A FILE.  `lpq', `prjob' and `lp' look fora DATA MODULE called `spoolqueue' in memory and answer `no spoolerinstalled' when it is not there.  Starting `splman &' does not createit -- splman runs, as a daemon should, and lpq still says no spooler.Measured 2026-08-29.`lpsched' DOES BUILD IT, and the line that used to stand here -- thatwhatever builds `spoolqueue' is not on this disk -- was wrong.Measured 2026-09-20:    lpsched /nil &     forks prjob and stays resident, waiting in F$Event    edir -e            lists the event `spoolqueue', value 0    (the emulator's own module list) shows a `spoolqueue' data module    lpq                still answers `no spooler installed'    lpshut             still answers `no spooler active'    lp <file>          still answers `Spooler not installed'So the queue exists, in both the forms the set uses, and all threeclient programs disagree.  What their installed-test actually is hasNOT been established -- there is no source on this disk for any ofthem.  Name your own printer in place of /nil and try it on a systemwith a printer before concluding anything; the whole set may simplywant one.